\description{
Reports what a dataset contains and whether it has one record per key
variable combination. This is useful after a derivation, e.g. to check that
\code{\link[=derive_param_computed]{derive_param_computed()}} added the records that were expected.
}
\details{
The summary always shows the dataset name and ADaM class, the number of
subjects, observations and variables, and the record structure. It then adds
the information for the dataset class (see \code{\link[=get_dataset_class]{get_dataset_class()}}):
\itemize{
\item \code{ADSL}: subjects per treatment arm, the population flag counts, the subject
flow (randomized, treated, completed), the treatment duration, the end of
study status, the deaths and their causes, and the age and sex of the
subjects.
\item \code{BDS}/\code{MDBDS}: records, subjects, visits, and the distribution of \code{AVAL}
per parameter, the analysis visits, and the derived records per \code{DTYPE}.
\item \code{OCCDS}/\code{MDOCCDS}: the number of distinct terms, and the
treatment-emergent vs total records.
\item \code{TTE}/\code{MDTTE}: events and censored per parameter.

The summary reports facts only, apart from the record structure check.
Each item is only reported when the variables it needs are in the dataset,
so a dataset which is still being derived gives a shorter summary rather
than an error.

The record structure is checked against the keys stored by \code{\link[=admiral_df]{admiral_df()}}.
Keys are never inferred, so if none were stored the structure is not
checked.

Subjects are counted by \code{USUBJID} if it is present, as the other subject
keys (e.g. \code{STUDYID}) may not be populated on records added by a
derivation. Otherwise the subject keys
(\code{get_admiral_option("subject_keys")}) are used.

The dataset name is the one stored by \code{\link[=admiral_df]{admiral_df()}} or, failing that, the
name of the variable \code{summary()} was called on.
}
